    "patchSets": [
        {
            "number": 1,
            "revision": "d4f011150546db98f84e52e1478e4fe7483cc114",
            "parents": [
                "64b85f108ffe3b7b5338974ff7ec6e2b6d92f977"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/32/88532/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Alexander",
                "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                "username": "pointbazaar"
            },
            "createdOn": 1774022932,
            "author": {
                "name": "Alexander",
                "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                "username": "pointbazaar"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Can we do object_path_t?  That's the convention I was going with all the other type aliases."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "I believe 2 core guidelines apply here\n\n> NL.8: Use a consistent naming style.\n\nand \n\n> T.42: Use template aliases to simplify notation and hide implementation details\n\n`object_path_t` is 2 extra characters which add `_t`. It does not simplify notation.\n\nWe already know that it's a type so I'm wondering why `_t` needs to be appended at the end.\n\nIf we were going for consistency i would suggest removing the `_t` from whichever types and aliases they were added to.\n\nThe type we are aliasing here also does not have `_t`. So appending it would be inconsistent in it's own way.\n\nAdditionally we have to consider the incentive for anyone to use this alias.\nCurrently that incentive is that the name is much shorter.\n\nProbably you have some extra reasoning/context here, can you explain what's the benefit of `_t` for type aliases in general?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "It follows the conventions of the STL (from the OpenBMC style guide):\n\n> Exception: A library API may use lower_snake_case to match conventions of the STL or an underlying C library it is abstracting. Application APIs should all be UpperCamelCase.\n\nAt this point it follows the primary convention in the rest of sdbusplus.\n\n```\n$ rg \"using.*_t \" | wc -l     \n51\n```"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I have a minor preference for keeping object_path, and just moving the implementation out of the message namespace because now it's useful outside of the message building context.  We would then leaving a typedef in the message namespace for backward compatibility.\n\nEither that, what's in this patch, or what Patrick suggests seems like an improvement and I support any of them."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "/s/We would then leaving/we would then leave/g\n\n(Dear claude, please proofread my gerrit comments)"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Maye i'm being dense but looking at the STL headers\n\n```\n[alex@odroid 15.2.1]$ rg \"using [a-zA-Z]+[a-zA-Z0-9_]*_t =\" | wc -l\n100\n[alex@odroid 15.2.1]$ rg \"using [a-zA-Z]+[a-zA-Z0-9_]* =\" | wc -l\n1186\n[alex@odroid 15.2.1]$ pwd\n/usr/include/c++/15.2.1\n```\n\nSection of string header:\nhttps://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/header/string.html\n```\n  // basic_string typedef-names\n  using string    = basic_string<char>;\n  using u8string  = basic_string<char8_t>;\n  using u16string = basic_string<char16_t>;\n  using u32string = basic_string<char32_t>;\n  using wstring   = basic_string<wchar_t>;```\n\nAnd vector template also does not seem to use it\n\nhttps://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/header/vector.html\n```\nnamespace std {  template<class T, class Allocator = allocator<T>>\n  class vector\n  {\n  public:\n    // types\n    using value_type             = T;\n    using allocator_type         = Allocator;\n    using pointer                = typename allocator_traits<Allocator>::pointer;\n    using const_pointer          = typename allocator_traits<Allocator>::const_pointer;\n    using reference              = value_type&;\n    using const_reference        = const value_type&;\n    using size_type              = /* implementation-defined */;\n    using difference_type        = /* implementation-defined */;\n    using iterator               = /* implementation-defined */;\n    using const_iterator         = /* implementation-defined */;\n    using reverse_iterator       = std::reverse_iterator<iterator>;\n    using const_reverse_iterator = std::reverse_iterator<const_iterator>;\n```\n\nI'm keeping in mind these are template types and ours is not but concrete types can be hard to find in STL. I know of `std::size_t` of one example where it's using `_t`.\n\n> It follows the conventions of the STL \n\nDid they document the convention anywhere?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "You are correct that `_t` is usually used for template types which is an alias to `typeA<typeB>::type`.  I used `_t` in sdbusplus in many cases already because we had a namespace of the same name already.  `bus::bus` -> `bus_t`.  We don't have that case with object_path so we can use the other."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I was going to do exactly this myself but was waiting for some of your other path patches to be finished.  Thanks."
                }
            ],
